Golem: a new tile splits the window where the pointer is, on every screen.

With dwindle:use_active_for_splits off (Golem, 2026-11-17: a new window opens
beside the window under the pointer, on its side, and nothing is focused for
it), stock decides whether the pointer counts by asking if the new window's
workspace is the one shown on the FOCUSED screen. When it is not (another
screen has the focus, or the workspace is not showing: an app that opens its
window on a desk you left, a slow app that maps after you moved to the other
screen), and also when the pointer is over no tile and not on a reserved edge,
stock splits the workspace's FIRST tile, the oldest window, which then halves
again with every such window.

Now the pointer counts where it is: over this workspace's own screen while
that screen shows it, the tile under it or else the tile nearest it. With the
pointer elsewhere, or the workspace not showing, the new tile splits the
window last used on that workspace (else the tile nearest the pointer). A
window moved with a focal point (a drop, a move between workspaces) and
use_active_for_splits on are stock. With force_split 0 the new tile takes the
pointer's side only when the pointer is on that screen; otherwise it goes
right or below. The new window is never its own split target: one brought
back from another workspace (Golem's minimize, a move) kept its old box, often
under the pointer, and stock then fell to the first tile too. Golem's own;
keep.

